<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<phpunit xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://schema.phpunit.de/10.5/phpunit.xsd" bootstrap="tests/bootstrap.php" colors="true" cacheDirectory=".phpunit.cache" executionOrder="random" failOnRisky="true" failOnWarning="true" beStrictAboutOutputDuringTests="true" displayDetailsOnTestsThatTriggerDeprecations="true" displayDetailsOnTestsThatTriggerWarnings="true" displayDetailsOnTestsThatTriggerErrors="true">
  <testsuites>
    <testsuite name="Unit">
      <directory suffix="Test.php">tests/Unit</directory>
    </testsuite>
    <testsuite name="Integration">
      <directory suffix="Test.php">tests/Integration</directory>
    </testsuite>
    <testsuite name="E2E">
      <directory suffix="Test.php">tests/E2E</directory>
    </testsuite>
  </testsuites>
  <groups>
    <exclude>
      <group>network</group>
    </exclude>
  </groups>
  <!--
    S141 — HOW TO READ THIS REPO'S COVERAGE NUMBERS. Read this before concluding
    that a 0.00% file is untested.

    The `<source>` block below is clean: every `src/` file appears in the clover.
    The narrowing happens per-TEST instead. 358 of the 695 test files carry a
    `@covers` annotation, and PHPUnit restricts each such test's recorded coverage
    to the units it names — everything else that test executes is DISCARDED, with
    no warning. So "this code is untested" and "this code's coverage was thrown
    away by an annotation" are indistinguishable in the report.

    Measured A/B on PHP 8.3.6 + PCOV 1.0.11, one test file, one annotation, nothing
    else changed (`tests/Unit/Server/Core/MusicTracksRouteReachabilityTest.php`,
    which builds a Router, registers routes through it and reads them back):

      with    @covers \Phlix\Server\Core\Application
        Phlix\Server\Http\Router   Lines:  0.00% (  0/210)   — absent from the report
      with the annotation removed
        Phlix\Server\Http\Router   Lines: 13.33% ( 28/210)

    Whole-run statements for that file went 22 -> 130: the annotation discarded 83%
    of what the test actually executed. This reproduces the original S141
    measurement (MusicLibraryService.php 0.00% -> 16.04%) on an unrelated pair.

    AUTHORITATIVE READING, until the project-wide policy decision in S141 is made:
      * A non-zero figure is real and is a LOWER BOUND.
      * A 0.00% figure means "no test NAMES this unit in a @covers" — it does NOT
        mean no test executes it. Confirm by re-running the suspected test with its
        @covers removed BEFORE writing new tests for that file, or the suite grows
        duplicate tests that pin nothing new.
      * The headline percentage, and therefore the DEFAULT_MIN_COVERAGE = 40.0 floor
        in scripts/coverage-threshold-check.php, are UNDERSTATED by an unmeasured
        margin. The floor is still meaningful as a ratchet; it is not an estimate of
        how much of this codebase is exercised.

    The open decision (S141): keep @covers everywhere and accept the narrowing, or
    drop it and read whole-run coverage. It is not recorded here because it has not
    been made — this comment records the MEASUREMENT and the reading, so that a 0%
    is at least an explained zero rather than a silent one.
  -->
  <coverage>
    <report>
      <html outputDirectory="coverage-report"/>
      <clover outputFile="coverage.xml"/>
      <text outputFile="php://stdout" showUncoveredFiles="true" showOnlySummary="true"/>
    </report>
  </coverage>
  <extensions>
    <!--
      S120 — reports any test where a failed assertion did not decide the test's
      outcome (an assertion swallowed by a `catch (\Throwable)` / `catch (\RuntimeException)`
      between the assertion and PHPUnit). See
      tests/Support/AssertionEscape/EscapeCollector.php for the mechanism, for the
      failure paths that emit no event and are therefore invisible to it
      (Assert::fail(), mock parameter/invocation rules, markTestSkipped/Incomplete),
      and for the one known false-positive class (a test that deliberately catches its
      own assertion failure) together with the reasoning for providing no opt-out.
      scripts/assertion-escape-check.php turns a report into a non-zero exit for CI.
    -->
    <bootstrap class="Phlix\Tests\Support\AssertionEscape\AssertionEscapeGuardExtension"/>
  </extensions>
  <php>
    <env name="APP_ENV" value="testing"/>
    <env name="DB_HOST" value="127.0.0.1"/>
    <env name="DB_PORT" value="3306"/>
    <env name="DB_DATABASE" value="phlix_test"/>
    <env name="DB_USER" value="root"/>
    <env name="DB_PASSWORD" value="root"/>
  </php>
  <source>
    <include>
      <directory suffix=".php">src</directory>
    </include>
    <exclude>
      <directory suffix=".php">src/vendor</directory>
    </exclude>
  </source>
</phpunit>
